WEBVTT


00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.970
When joining panels of knitting, instead
of trying to hide the seam, draw attention

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:07.510
to it.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.610
Our knitting machines limit us to the
number of stitches we can knit at a time.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.940
So sometimes we have to add
a seam on a garment piece.

00:00:16.815 --> 00:00:21.345
For example, the Knit it Now
Basics patterns for larger sizes.

00:00:21.555 --> 00:00:25.875
Calculate a full back of
a cardigan into pieces.

00:00:26.895 --> 00:00:33.855
Now you may be an expert finisher, but I
dunno about you, stitching up a seam down

00:00:33.855 --> 00:00:36.405
the center back of a
sweater really annoys me.

00:00:37.595 --> 00:00:42.214
Instead of hiding the seam, why
not make it a design element?

00:00:42.925 --> 00:00:46.855
You may be familiar with
a hairpin lace join.

00:00:47.095 --> 00:00:52.824
We often use this to join blanket panels
and this'll work for your sweater, but

00:00:52.824 --> 00:00:55.944
there are so many more things you can do.

00:00:56.995 --> 00:01:03.685
How about letting that seam show on the
public side of your garment and just join

00:01:03.685 --> 00:01:05.185
it with single crochet.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:11.770
You'll place the wrong sides together and
single crochet through half a stitch on

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.120
each edge of your knitting.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:19.230
You don't crochet, hang both sides on
the machine with the wrong or the private

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.490
sides together.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.729
Hang half a stitch and bind off
fairly loosely around the gate pegs.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:31.830
You can always knit the second
panel with a sew-as-you-go method.

00:01:32.475 --> 00:01:36.165
But try it with your yarn and
your stitch pattern first.

00:01:36.705 --> 00:01:42.135
If your stitch pattern is very textured,
this might blend in beautifully and it'll

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:44.745
save you finishing that seam.

00:01:46.415 --> 00:01:51.135
Let's really draw attention
to the scene in this sample.

00:01:51.135 --> 00:01:56.755
I transferred the second stitch to the
third every other row creating an eyelet.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:05.850
In other words, I left a seaming stitch in
work and transfer this stitch next to it

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.890
to form an eyelet on the edge.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:15.850
When I knit the adjoining piece, I simply
hung these eyelets on the edge needle

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.270
every other row joining the two pieces.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.870
This time as I joined that eyelet edge.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:30.950
Like before I created an eyelett on the
joining piece, creating two columns of

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.880
eyelets along the edge.

00:02:42.385 --> 00:02:45.085
Now take this idea one step further.

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:52.045
Use your three prong transfer tool and
create a full fashion transfer to decorate

00:02:52.045 --> 00:02:54.325
this eyelet edge even more.

00:02:58.155 --> 00:03:03.225
As you create the eyelets on working this
technique, make sure that the emptied

00:03:03.225 --> 00:03:06.945
needle is back in working
position each time.

00:03:10.115 --> 00:03:14.435
Maybe I'm getting carried away, but
putting a needle out of work adds even

00:03:14.435 --> 00:03:15.755
more interest.

00:03:17.015 --> 00:03:17.405
Hmm.

00:03:17.405 --> 00:03:21.605
Let's think we could put some
eyelets along that edge as well.

00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:32.859
Play with this technique.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:38.260
I'm sure you'll come up with something
that will decorate that seam and you'll be

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.160
proud to wear a seam down the
middle of the back of your sweater.

